<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhumij people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bhumij are an Adivasi people of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, living mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>. Speaking a Munda language closely related to Mundari, they traditionally farmed in the hills and forests of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chota-nagpur-plateau">Chota Nagpur</a> plateau. In 1832–1833 Bhumij of the Jungle Mahals rose under Ganga Narayan Singh against British-backed landlords and officials.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhumij-people.jpg" alt="Bhumij people" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sumita Roy Dutta · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Firkal_slowly_decaying_martial_dance_of_ChotaNagpur_of_Jharkhand_performed_at_Central_Park_Delhi_IMG_0934_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Bhumij means one who is born from the soil, from the word bhūmi, land or soil. N. Ramaswani read it instead as people of the Bhum areas, such as Singhbhum, Dhalbhum, Manbhum and Barabhum, and Dalton held the Bhumij to be the original inhabitants of Dhalbhum, Barabhum, Patkum and Baghmundi. A genetic study of 2010 found that about 70 per cent of their Y haplogroups belong to O2a-M95, common among Austroasiatic speakers and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, and their mitochondrial lineages to haplogroup M of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Those living nearer the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chota-nagpur-plateau">Chota Nagpur Plateau</a> kept close linguistic links with Mundari, while those further east and south took up Bengali and Odia, and in Dhalbhum they became wholly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> in practice. Under British rule, or earlier, many Bhumij became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a>, some gained the title of Raja and others were called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardar">Sardar</a>, and those who rose claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a> status, as was the trend in the region.</p><p>Bhumij landholders supported the Chuar revolt, a series of peasant risings between 1766 and 1816 in the country around Midnapore, Bankura and Manbhum against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s land revenue policies. In March 1766 the government sent an expedition west of Midnapore to force payment and demolish strongholds. Among the dispossessed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a> who backed the rebels were Jagannath Singh of Dhalbhum, Durjan Singh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raipur">Raipur</a>, Rani Shiromani of Karnagarh and Ganga Narayan of Barabhum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Bhumij society combines traits inherited from the Munda with elements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> society, and colonial writers often called it Hinduised. It is marked by the nuclear family, descent in the male line, marriage outside the clan and hereditary village headship, and it is divided into endogamous groups by ancestral territory and occupation. The Bhumij language, written in the Ol Onal script, is spoken by around 100,000 people and became a second official language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a> in January 2019. Followers of Sarnaism among them have petitioned for their religion to be recognised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. They are a Scheduled Tribe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, Jharkhand and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bhumij people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mayurbhanj had a large Santal, Ho and <b>Bhumij</b> population and is the home of the Mayurbhanj Chhau dance.</blockquote><a href="/state/mayurbhanj-state">Mayurbhanj State</a></li></ul></section>
